\item add more tension, such as by stretching them,
\end {enumerate}

Now test your predictions in (a) and (b). Record all the results from this activity in words and numbers. Come to conclusions about how to alter the pitch produced by these objects. Share your findings with the class. Can they confirm your results? Explain why we can hear some high-pitched sounds but not others. Give examples where low- pitched sounds are used instead of higher ones. Suggest reasons why people use whistles rather than shouting to communicate over long distances. Describe two situations where animals make low grunting noises to one another. Explain why birds do not also grunt like that. Why is it better if cats purr rather than bark at one another? Tell the class three things you have learned today. Choose something that was quite new information for you and explain it clearly so everyone understands. Now choose anything else you found interesting – perhaps surprising or useful. Finally tell us one thing you could improve upon in terms of changes made to the equipment you investigated. What would help someone listening who cannot see any movements being made? Think about making music. How might you change the pitch there? The volume? Say whether each statement below is true or false based on what happened during our investigations into sound. Write ‘True’ or ‘False’ after each sentence.
