<<74 Prompt>> Give me a question exercise for a 3rd grade student learning social studies. It should be an exercise on climate change. It should be an exercise on human impact on the environment. <<Prompt>>
<<74 Generation>> How do you think people can affect the natural world? Give three examples of how this happens in your country and say what problems it causes. Suggest some solutions to these problems. Present your ideas as a poster or short report. Think about using images, graphs and charts that show changes over time. Try to make recommendations at different scales – local (in school), regional/national and global/international. Be sure to consider both mitigation and adaptation measures when making suggestions. Explain why individual action is important even if collective action by many individuals will have more effect. Say which type of actions are best suited to being taken individually rather than collectively. Finally explain briefly why government policies are needed to address issues related to environmental protection. What kind of policy might help reduce littering? Why would such policies not work without law enforcement? Provide one example from your own experience where you tried to mitigate climate change effects but failed because there was no proper regulation/legislation/law regarding this issue. Reflect on how you could improve your efforts so they become more successful in the future. How does your knowledge influence your behaviour towards the environment now compared with before studying this subject? Describe two situations where you changed your behavior concerning consumption after becoming aware of the importance of reducing waste.
\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item Identify ways in which humans negatively impact the environment.
\item Propose potential solutions to environmental problems caused by human activity.
\end{enumerate} <<Generation>>
<<75 Prompt>> Give me a short answer exercise for a 3rd grade student learning social studies. It should be an exercise on consumerism. <<Prompt>>
<<75 Generation>> Identify three situations in which people are consumers and describe what is being consumed. Explain why it needs to be used carefully. Suggest how the careful use of these things can help society or improve life now, or make better lives in the future. Think about food, petrol, electricity/water/paper. Find out where each one comes from. Describe ways in which they could be wasted and suggest ideas that might reduce waste. Do some research into recycling schemes operating locally – find at least two examples. Present your findings orally. Write down any rules you know concerning littering and disposing of dangerous rubbish such as batteries, aerosols, medicine bottles etc. Tell the class if there are places near your home where litter accumulates and say what kinds of items tend to accumulate most often (look especially for plastics). Be ready with suggestions about solving this problem. Identify products made from recycled materials available in shops today. Give the class examples. Work together as a group to plan information sheets telling others what happens when certain types of product packaging are not disposed of properly. Include milk cartons, plastic drinks containers, glass jars/bottles/tins/foil packets/cigarette packs; margarine tubs; newspaper/magazines/junk mail; cardboard boxes/kitchen/yard/garden/glass/plastic/Metal/Foil/Paper/Cardboard. Make a list of all the different sorts of container brought back to school after break times during a week. Draw bar charts showing relative amounts of different categories collected over the days. Which kind was returned every day? Label your chart ‘Break-time Waste’. Show it to the rest of the class. Can anyone explain patterns shown by your results? What conclusions do you draw about making less waste? Suggest three changes to behaviour among pupils that would cut down classroom waste significantly. Are there enough bins around the school? Where are the worst areas? Should we have more bins? Would having more bins really solve our problems? Sift through waste from classrooms, empty the paper-recycling box contents, kitchen scraps and look at other non-identified collections. Sort them into different groups then present your observations orally. Collect six identical pieces of packaging, e.g., yoghurt pots or egg boxes. Try various methods to squash, crush or crumple them so they take up much less space than before. Test how well each method works using equal numbers of item. Compare the bulkiness of each set afterwards. Use the best way to prepare another collection of twelve items like those above and store them under your desk/in your drawer/or somewhere else until your next lesson. How many fit easily in their storage place? At the start of your next activity, retrieve them. Estimate first then weigh the total weight of the items prepared last time. Now work out the difference between estimated weights and real ones. Who had closest estimates? Was everyone's estimate higher/lower/thoroughly accurate compared to actual weight? How does compacting affect the amount of room needed to hold certain wastes? Is it worth doing? Compact some waste again but this time compare its weight before and after preparation – show it on a table/diagram. Does crushing help here too? In what circumstances will/do won't/could it help? When will it definitely make no difference either way? Say why you think so based on experiences and evidence gathered throughout activities done previously within this unit. Finally share reasons behind choosing specific kinds of packing material originally - perhaps thinking about durability/reusability/flexibility/lightness/ease usage/nature friendliness? Summarise points discussed regarding advantages/disadvantages relating volume versus mass reductions achieved via compaction techniques applied across sampled goods! Then evaluate practicality feasibility implementation viability effectiveness generalisability environmental impact potentia