<<83 Generation>> A lorry driver took 5 hours to travel from his depot to Warehouse C and back. His average speed for the whole journey was 80 km/h. If he travelled at an average speed of 70 km/h for \( \frac{2}{7} \) of the total time, find his average speed (in km/h) for the rest of the journey.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item What is the formula for calculating average speed?
\item How do you calculate the distance traveled by the lorry?
\end{enumerate}

Let's break it down step-by-step:

\[
\text{Average Speed} = \frac{\text{Total Distance}}{\text{Tota Time}}
\]

Given:
- Total Time = 5 h
- Average Speed = 80 kph

To find the total distance, we rearrange the formula as follows:

\[ 
\text {Total Distance }= \text {average speed } \times \text{total time }
\] 

Now substitute in values to get your answer:

\begin {equation*}
   \text{{Total Dist }}= \underbrace{(80)(5)}_{400\,\text{kilometres}}.
\end {equatiom*}  

The second part asks us what the truck’s average speed would have been if only one-seventh of this trip were done at 70 kilometres per hour instead.

