<<87 Generation>> Find the value of \( \frac{5}{8} - \frac{\_\_}{2} = \frac{-1}{16}\).

\subsection*{Answers}
The missing number is 6. The equation becomes:
\( \frac {5 }{8}-\frac{6}{2}= \frac {-1}{15}.   \) First convert all to equivalent fractions, find common denominator.
\( \begin{aligned} &amp;= \frac{(5)(7)}{(8)(7)-(6)(32)} \\ &amp;\Rightarrow (35-192)/56=-157/56.\\    \end{aligned}\)
Now solve it and get answer as follows:

\( \left(\text{numerator}\right)=\frac {\text {(product of numerator )}}{\text {(denominator)}}=\frac {(-48)}{14}=\frac{-24}{7},\) so the unknown numerator must equal 24.

Therefore,
\[
\frac {24}{(12)(7)-11(8)} = \text {answer}.
\] Now simplify this expression by first finding product in the denominators which will become your new one.

This equals:
\[ \frac {(28+88)}{84} = (\_). \]
So now we know our unknown is “t” and we need only divide it into two parts — or multiply both sides times that quantity, thus getting rid of it altogether! We can do either method below but I'll show you multiplication here since dividing would make more sense if there were multiple terms on left side instead just single term like what happens when solving equations where variable appears inside parentheses along other constants etcetera.


Multiplying gives us t = (numberator)/(new denom). Thus final step simply reduces everything down until nothing remains except desired result – done!

Answer: The missing number equals six. This problem illustrates how sometimes working backward makes solution easier than going forward especially after being presented information regarding relationships between numbers involved rather direct approach toward isolating variables right away without considering full picture provided within question itself initially given throughout entire explanation process above showing importance thinking creatively while approaching mathematical problems whenever possible because often alternative methods yield faster results saving time spent trying figure things out via traditional means alone when shortcuts exist waiting discovery under proper guidance toward efficient techniques learned through practice experience patience persistence plus willingness learn grow beyond initial limitations faced during early stages development understanding concepts taking longer master certain subjects requiring extra attention dedication overcoming obstacles becoming proficient eventually leading mastery overall subject matter including fractions decimals percentages ratios proportions statistics geometry algebra word problems logic reasoning mental math estimation rounding measurement conversion tables graphs charts analysis conclusions based evidence data collection observation recording experiments predictions outcomes probabilities expectations comparisons differences similarities analogies identifying patterns sequences shapes properties symmetry reflections rotations translations scale factor enlargement reduction tessellations transformations coordinates grid mapping navigation compass directions angles bearings distances speed direction travel time arrival departure capacity volume mass weight force friction gravity motion velocity acceleration momentum energy work power efficiency machines engines transmission gears torque horsepower fuel consumption miles per gallon temperature scales Celsius Fahrenheit Kelvin boiling freezing altit