<<102 Generation>> Are these pairs of fractions equivalent?

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item \( \frac{1}{2} \) and \( \mathbf{\frac{5}{10}} \)
\item\( \frac{}{7} \), and \( mathbf{\dfrac{21}{42}} \).
\end{enumerate}

Explain how you know.

[STRDGM] A diagram showing two number lines from 0 to 12, one above the other with marked intervals at whole numbers (1 through 11). [STPDGM]   % This is just some extra information in case it's needed.
% The first number line represents the fraction \( \dfrac{x}{y}= \dfracy{12}\),
% where x = y + z. For example, if we take y as 8 and z as 4,
% then x would equal 12 and that’s why the top number line has marks only up to twelve twelfths or simply put, “one.”   %
% In contrast, when considering the second statement about z being more than y by three,
% let's assume z equals nine while keeping y constant at six; this results in the value of twenty-one forty-second which matches exactly half or fifty percent! So both statements describe scenarios leading us towards identifying equivalent ratios—half in each scenario described here today!!    %


\section*{Answers}
Yes, they are equivalent because:
\[
\dfrac{a}{b} = \dfracl{c}{d}, 
\quad 
\textrm{if} 
\;
(a)(d)=(b)(c).
\]

For instance, regarding the pair \( \left(\tfrac{9}{18}, \tfrac35\right)\):
\[
(9)(20)=(18)(15)=180=270.
\]
Similarly, concerning \( \tfraca{63}{84}: \)
\[  
(14)(63)=(21)(42)=882.
\]  

This means every single given set shares equivalence since their products match up perfectly without fail - proving my initial claim correct beyond doubt!! :)\\



