\subsection*{Answers}
Ik drink een kop kofﬁe.\\
Jij schrijft het artikel van Nederland.\\

I am drinking a cup of coffee.\\ 
You are writing this article about Holland.\\ 

(Note that the second sentence is not very natural, but it was chosen to demonstrate the form.)\\ 

In English you would probably say:\\ 
This is my article about holland. Or I've written an article about... etcetera. But we wanted to show you how to use the present tense when describing what someone or something is doing at any given moment. The same goes for the following sentences. They're all possible and quite commonly used, even though they might sound strange if translated literally into English!\\ 

For example:\\ 
Hij maakt de pizza's. He makes pizzas/He is making the pizzas.\\  Zij helpt ons. She helps us/She is helping us.\\   Mijn moeder leest dat boek. My mother reads/is reading that book.\\    Wij fietsen door de polder. We cycle through the dunes /We are cycling through the polders.\\     U spreekt Nederlands. You speak Dutch/You are speaking Dutch.\\      Zij spreken Engels. They speak English/They are speaking English.\\       De kinderen spelen met hun nieuwe poppen. The children play with their new dolls/The children are playing with...\\        Uw manieren zijn niet netjes. Your manners aren't polite/Your manner isn't nice.\\         Hij ziet alles. He sees everything/He can see everything.\\          Je draagt een nieuwe broek vandaag. You wear a new pair of trousers today./You are wearing a new... etceterer. And so forth. In many cases there will only be one way to translate these verbs from Dutch to English. Sometimes both options give sense, sometimes just one does. What do you think? Which ones could work either way?\\  How about this last lot then? Do you think some of them could be interpreted as 'can' rather than 'is'?\\  

De dijk houdt het water tegen. The dyke holds back the water/the dykes hold back the waters.\\  
Zit u lekker op uw stoel? Are you sitting comfortably in your chair?/Are you comfortable on your seat?\\  
Je bedoelt toch niet serieus dat dit je lievelingsboek is?! You don't mean that seriously, that this is your favourite book?!/Do you really mean that..?\\    
U bent te laat voor het feest. You are late for the party!/You were too late for....\\      
Ga jij naar huis nu? Are going home now?/Will you go home now?/Do go home right now! \\   
Kunnen jullie morgen komen? Can you come tomorrow?/Can you two come tomorrow??/Could you guys possibly make it tomorrow?? \\
Moet je echt zo hard werken? Must you really work so hard?/Is it necessary to work like that??/Don't you feel like working that much anymore??\\     
Werk je hier al lang? Work here long?/Have been working here for ages???/How long did you work here exactly???\\        
Draait hij zich om en om? Is he turning around and round?/Does he turn himself around and around??/Did he keep on spinning around????????\\       
Gaat ze vaak uit eten? Goes out eating often?? Does she regularly eat out?????/Has she gone out for dinner more frequently lately?????\\         
Ligt de post onderin de tas? Lies down under inside bag/Lies the letter at bottom of bag/Directly underneath in her handbag lies a small letter!!!!!!!!!!!!\\          
Verliest haar woorden bijna nooit. Loses words almost never!/Never loses his word!!/Almost never forgets what he wants to say!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!\\          

Think carefully before deciding whether each one sounds better with 'do', 'does','will,'must,''could''might'or whatever other auxiliary verb comes up first. Then look below for our suggestions. Keep thinking while looking at those - do agree or disagree? Still got questions after checking ours against yours? Ask yourself why certain translations seem more plausible than others. There may well be lots of things you haven't thought about yet!! Don't worry, because next time you'll find more and more of the translations sounding odd and less logical!! As usual, practice makes perfect! Good luck and don't get discouraged by some weirdness every once in awhile :). For instance;\\   

Might seems better suited in this case:
\begin {enumerate} 
\item Gaat ze vaal uit etan?
