\section{Extra Dialogue}
A dialogue between Leona and her friend Willemijn.
Leona: Hé Will, hoe gaat het?
Willemijn: Goed hoor en met jou?
Leona:Hetzelfde.
Willemjin:Werk je nu al een tijdje?
Leoma:Jee, ik ben nu ruim twee maanden bezig.
Willmijn:En wat vind je ervan?
Leonam: Het valt nog steeds tegen dat ik niet meer kan shoppen zoals vroeger maar verder gaat alles goed.
Willemin:Gelukkig, ik werk over een paar dagen ook weer, dus ik heb genoeg gespaard om lekker veel uit te gaan.
Leonm:Ja dat zal toch echt een keertje zijn!
Wilemn:(laughs)
Lemon:(laughs)

\subsection{Translation}
Leona:\ How are you?
Wilhelmine:Good and yourself?
Leoena:The same thing.
Wilhejmyn:Are you already working for sometime now?
Leeonm:Yes, I've started two months ago.
WiIlennine:So,how do you like it?
Leoonma:I still regret that I am no longer able to shop around like I use to, but otherwise everything is fine with me.
Wiilliemine:Lucky me! In few days Ill start working again so I saved quite enough money to hangout pretty much.
Lenoa:That must definitely be great fun though!!
(Wilhelmina laughs)
(Lenoa Laughs)\\[5mm]


Note: A number of words in this dialogue may seem unfamiliar to non-Dutch speakers because they contain abbreviations such as hoor, geht and Vali instead of hoort, gezegd and volgens respectively. These changes reflect actual spoken dialects found commonly among younger generations however remain absent from formal writing styles including news papers magazines etcetera. For better understanding purposes we included translations below original texts whenever necessary but kept using full versions during lessons themselves since those tend sound slightly unnatural without proper accent accompanying pronuncations therwise keeping distance between informal/formal dutch usage clear helps avoid confusions later future references back toward earlier learned phrases/sentences especially considering differences betweeen regional accents varying greatly across country even neighboring cities/towns sometimes having distinct local expressions unique only there thus giving priority accuracy essential maintaining integrity intended messages conveyed originally rather diluting impact adopting colloquialimsms here there throughout entire instruction process consistency key achieving fluency mastering languages.\\



