<<283 Generation>> Express the difference between two numbers as a percentage of one number.

\subsection*{Answers}
Let \( x \) and \( y \) represent the two different numbers where \( x > y \). The formula to express their difference (say, \( d = x - y \)) as a percent of \( x is:

\( \%_{x} = \frac{(d)}{x}\times100=\frac{x-y}{x}\)

Substituting values:
If we let \( x = \$60 \), then:
The answer will depend on what you choose for \( y. For example if \(y=30\) then the result would be: \( \frac{\$30-\$15}{\$30}=50% \text{of } \$30\) which equals \( \$15 \).

But in general, it depends on your choice of second value.
In this case,
\( \%_1=\left(\frac{a-b}{b}\right)\cdot100 \)
And also 
\( \%2=\left( \frac{c-d}{c}\right) \cdot100  \)

So, here are some examples below based on these formulas above using some sample data like this table showing differences between several pairs of percentages or ratios expressed in both ways mentioned before:

| Item | Value A (\%) | Value B (%) |
|------|--------------|-------------|
| Ratio1 |   20       |     10      |
| Ratio2 |    5        |      8       |

