When you take away zero how many are left? Complete this part-whole model by writing how many tens there are altogether:

There are \_\,\text{tens} altogether. That means there are \underline{20} counters altogether. So when you take one ten away (which equals ten counters), there will still be \underline{(20 - 10)} counters remaining, which also represents \underline{-}. To show this visually, we draw \underline{squares}, where each square stands for one counter. In summary, removing one ten leaves us with \_\,_ squares or \_\,. And if we were to remove another ten, we'd have no whole tens but only individual counters amounting to \underline{}. Thus, taking all tens out would result in having just those single units as our total count.



