<<381 Prompt>> Give me a combination of a word problem and an arithmetic exercise for a 3rd grade student learning mathematics. It should have multiple steps. <<Prompt>>
<<381 Generation>> Mrs. Lin paid \$83.50 for the basket of fish.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item How much did she pay per kilogram?
\end{enumerate}  

\subsection*{Answers}
She paid \$2.90 per kilo (to two decimal places). If you rounded to three decimal places, it would be \$2,907. However, in this context, rounding to two decimal place is more appropriate as currency does not go beyond two decimal point. Therefore, \( \frac{\$83}{28.75 \text{ kg}} = \$2,.90 \) or \( \$83 - 28.7 \times \$2.9 = \$0 \), which shows that both methods are correct. For accuracy purposes though, use method one. The reason being that if there were 100 kilograms at $2.91 each, then using your calculator you get:
\( 291000/10000 = 29.1 \).
But,
\( 2.9090909... \times 100 = 290.90909.... \)
In other words, always calculate first before multiplying by amount because sometimes due to rounding errors when dividing first, can give incorrect answers later on down the line! This might seem trivial but makes all difference between passing with flying colors versus barely scraping through during exams where every mark counts!!! So remember kids how you solve matters too! Not just what numbers look right when calculated out correctly following order operations properly keeping those extra digits until final step ensures correctness even after multiple calculations whereas otherwise may lead small mistakes add up over time causing grades suffer unnecessarily simply because shortcut taken here thought didn't matter much now do see why paying attention details like these really pays off long run? Good job sticking around till end explanation happy helping whenever questions arise keep asking stay curious world full amazing math secrets waiting discovery yours alone take charge never stop exploring wonderland numbers!!! And don’t forget practice makes perfect so make sure try similar problems home work through examples given classroom apply concepts learned new situations encountered daily life soon become master mathematician solving everyday mysteries hidden plain sight waiting clever minds unlock them!!! Now let’s wrap things nicely shall we review key points discussed ensure nothing missed along way… To find unit price divide total cost item number units purchased example finding cost fish Mrs Lin bought multiply her purchase weight type seafood chosen lets say salmon halibut cod etc lastly important note made earlier bears repeating when performing division especially involving money avoid premature rounding maintain precision intermediate calculation stages only round final answer required significant figures according question asked this prevent potential inaccuracies creeping into solution presenting misleading information another crucial aspect consider di