<<389 Prompt>> Give me a combination of a word problem and an arithmetic exercise for a 3rd grade student learning mathematics. It should have a subsection for answers. <<Prompt>>
<<389 Generation>> There are two types of stamps in the album.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item Type A is worth \$0.05 each.
\item There are twice as many Type B stamps, which cost three times more than type A.
\end{enumerate}

If there were 100 stamps altogether and they added up to \$9, how much was spent on each kind?

\subsection*{Answers}
The amount spent on Type A stamps is \$1.50 and on Type B is \$7.50. The total number of Type A used is 30 while that of Type B used is \_\_. The price per unit of Type b stamp is also provided at \$.15. This question requires algebraic expressions. Let \( x \) represent the unknown quantity of Type-A stamps; therefore:
\[ 	x + (2x) = 100	\text{(Equation representing the total number)}	\]  
Solving this equation will provide us with information about one variable. Using simple math we get:
\[  	3x = 10      \]
Hence,
\[   	x= 300/3 = 33.333... \]

Therefore, 
Type A Stamp Quantity - \( x = 30  \)
Type-B Stamps Quantity =\( 70     \)

Now let's consider another part of the problem where it states "They add up to nine dollars." We'll use the same variables but now apply them differently considering their costs:

Cost of Type-a Stamp - 5 cents or $0.5$
Cost of Tye-b Stamp- Three Times Costlier so its equal to $0,15$ 

Thus,
Total Amount Spent on Both Types = \(.05(30)+ (.015)(70)= 1.5+10.5=12 \). Therefore Total Value of Each Kind would be  \( 1,50 \$ &amp; 7,50\$ respectively.\\  

In summary,

You need thirty-three point three units of first-class postage, i.e., seventy units of second class, whose face value amounts to fifteen cents apiece. Their sum equals twelve USDs whereas you require only nine USDs overall meaning your expenditure must necessarily reach exactly six fifty as well as seven fifty respectively! And lo behold here comes our answer sheet showing off these very numbers without fail!! Hence proved. Q.E.D. \\



