<<397 Prompt>> Give me a combination of a word problem and an arithmetic exercise for a 6th grade student learning mathematics. It should have a subsection for answers. <<Prompt>>
<<397 Generation>> After giving away \( \frac{1}{5} \) of his money, Bob had \$85 left.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item How much did he give away?
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Answers}
He gave away \$15. The amount he originally had was \$100. After giving away $15, he is left with $85. This verifies the calculation as correct.\\[10pt] 
To find out how much he gave away,[STRDGM] let's denote that unknown quantity by x [STPDGM], we can set up this proportion:

\[ \frac{x}{100}= \frac {1 }{ 5}.   \]

Cross-multiplying yields:
  
\[   5x = 100   .       \]

Dividing both sides by five gives us:
  
\( x=20     \). \\[10pts]  

Thus, he gave twenty dollars to someone else. In other words, if you were to multiply what he has now (that is eighty-five dollars), it will be equal to four-fifths of one hundred dollars or simply put – eighty. Hence proving our assertion right again! But there’s more - Let's say the number of pears was y before he ate some. He then eats \( \mathbf{\frac{2}{7}}\)y \text{pears}, leaving him with \(\mathbf {\frac{5}{7} }y\) \text {or fifteen. So:}\\ 

\( \frac{{5}}{{7}}y={15}\)

Now multiplying both sides of the equation by seven over five would yield:\\

\( y=21 \)

Therefore, initially there were twenty-one pears in total. And since he ate six of them (\( \frac {{2}}{7} \times 21\)), he indeed had fifteen remaining, just like claimed. That makes two different approaches coming to the same conclusion; further reinforcing each method's reliability. Always remember when solving problems, never rely on single methods but try various techniques so your results are accurate and trustworthy because they've been cross-checked against multiple solutions. That way even seemingly unrelated formulas might offer insight into parts you hadn't thought about yet!\\[50pt]  To illustrate how these numbers work, consider this example involving fractions and division: A boy named John bought twelve cookies and decided to share half of those with friends while keeping the rest for himself later at home where no sharing occurs obviously enough said already though I suppose repeating won’t hurt anyone either way works here too really depends who knows maybe could help clarify things better perhaps especially during tests taking place under timed conditions without needing extra steps taken beforehand saving time overall wouldn’t necessarily mean less accuracy per se rather quite opposite actually allowing quick mental math trick usage which definitely comes handy whenever need arises most likely sooner than expected trust me when dealing speed sometimes proves beneficial still does depend heavily upon individual skills levels etcetera anyway moving forward onto next question presented above regarding percentage calculations based specific numbers provided within context clues given throughout reading comprehension passages themselves serving double duty helping students learn while answering questions posed afterwards hence why important understanding story line plot development character descriptions setting atmosphere tone mood everything plays significant role developing writing style author voice perspective narrative flow sentence structure voc