<<516 Generation>> Mrs Li had \$2040 in the bank. She withdrew some money yesterday and her account balance was reduced to \$1860.

How much did she withdraw? \_\_\_ dollars were withdrawn from Mrs Li's account. The answer is \_\_. I calculated it by subtracting 1860 from 2040, which equals \_\_, then I took away another ten, so my calculation is correct. My method is correct because when you take away (subtract) you are finding the difference between two numbers. You can use any number that has a zero as the units digit or tens digit, like this example with 204 and 186. When there isn't one you could regroup the tens into ones, but taking off multiples of ten makes subtraction easier! For instance, \( 73 - 30 = 43 \). It helps make estimation quicker too – try estimating how many items fit within certain quantities before counting them individually; doing multiple takes less time than adding each individual item every single day after work/school etc.! Estimating also reduces errors made during calculations since approximations provide checkpoints throughout problems where exact answers aren’t required immediately at first glance without further thought put towards solving overall question asked originally presented here today regarding withdrawal amount based upon provided balances shown above now concluded below following explanation given prior demonstrating step-by-step reasoning utilized determining solution found next sentence:

