\paragraph{Bonus Question}
How will you know if this part of the program works? Describe how you could test it, either by writing it down or explaining orally. Think about what inputs you'd give the program so that this piece is used. Then think about what output you expect from running this code snippet based on those inputs. Does the actual output match what was expected? If yes, then it's working correctly! Otherwise, there might be something wrong with your program. This process helps ensure everything functions as intended before moving forward into more complex parts or sharing them publicly where others can utilize these tools too - always double-check first because fixing problems early saves time later during development stages; remember testing isn't just seeing whether things work but also making sure they don’t break anything else along their path toward completion!! Now go ahead – put together some test cases following above guidelines describing both successful ones proving functionality AND intentionally incorrect examples showing robustness against false positives/negatives alike since knowing limits matters equally much here compared success stories afterall!!!  

