<<561 Generation>> Discuss with a friend what you would do if there was no water in the taps when you went to school on Monday morning, or at home that evening? What would be your biggest problem? How long could you manage without running water from the tap? Where else might you get some water? Do you think bottled drinking water is expensive? Why does it cost so much more than ordinary tap water? Would people pay as much money for water if they did not have to buy it all the time? Explain why you think this. Now discuss how you will use less water tomorrow. Write down three ways each. Share them with the class. Can anyone add any extra ideas? Collect together four empty plastic bottles. Use these containers to collect dripping taps over one day. Measure how many litres (or millilitres) of drips are collected by night-time! Tell the class about it next lesson. Find out where the mains water comes into your house. Who has seen the meter box? If nobody knows ask an adult member of your family. Is it indoors or outside? Has anybody ever read the meter? Ask adults which months their bills show highest consumption of water – winter or summer? Suggest two reasons why this happens. Which countries around the world face severe problems because of lack of clean water supplies? Try using reference books such as atlases or encyclopedias to help find answers. Choose one country and prepare to tell the rest of the class something about its difficulties due to shortages of water. Your teacher may wish to mark your work. Work out whether you can see patterns between:
