You should notice that most cities are along the banks of rivers. People living in deserts cannot grow crops unless they irrigate them. This means bringing water up through pipes underground, under pressure, and then spraying it onto the growing plants. This uses large quantities of water especially since some evaporates before reaching the roots. Water flows downhill naturally towards sea level but needs pumping uphill against gravity to reach fields above the river. It costs energy and therefore money to pump this irrigation water to higher places like farms near towns and cities built high above the surrounding countryside. In Egypt's case, cities are usually very low down and often actually below sea-level so the water does not need lifting far upwards. However, even here lots of precious fuel oil is burnt in generating electricity driving the pumps delivering life-giving water via channels leading away from the main stream flowing northwards towards the Mediterranean Sea. A new dam completed recently holds back enormous volumes of stored-up water. Hydro-electricity generated here powers giant spinning machines propelling huge fans forcing air into the atmosphere creating artificial winds which drive ships laden with grain grown thanks to these waters past rocky shallows blocking navigation further upstream. The windmills lower down the course were made redundant. They had been crucial previously blowing air gently pushing sail boats carrying foodstuffs downstream towards hungry mouths waiting in Cairo and Alexandria for centuries until replaced recently by modern vessels using diesel motors instead. Thus the latest Aswan High Dam has solved both famine and congestion simultaneously freeing sailors' lives spent for generations blowing whistles trying vainly to alert passengers to dangers ahead hidden beneath surfaces calm deceptively yet swirling strongly enough drag anchors underwater connected loosely to ships floating unsuspectingly slowly onwards towards the unseen hazards threatening destruction only avoidable sometimes by loud warning blasts hopefully forewarning those aboard drifting unawares sleepily towards disaster only just barely avoided following frantic whistle-blowings from experienced old salts standing watchfully at helms steering safely round sharp jutting rocks suddenly materialising out of depths tranquilly disguising turbulent whirlpools churning powerfully just beneath gentle-looking flow-on of peaceful currents proceeding quietly seawards in search of outlets finally reached after thousands kilometres journeyed persistently from fountainheads bubbling forth crystal-clear mountain springs far distant beyond horizons curving earthwards invisibly merging seamlessly cloudy skies reflecting shimmering sunlit waves dancing joyously heavenward casting rippling shadows darkening mysterious deeps hiding secrets untold beneath glassy smoothnesses unruffled by rough storms subsiding softly leaving peaceful silence reigning supreme once again till next turbulence stirs far-off ripples disturbing quietude stillness restored reigned till eternity eventually turning brackish meeting ocean-salts diluting freshwater sources gradually disappearing fathomless blue abysses swallowing soundlessly mighty rivers sinking silently swallowed whole by insatiable seas gaping open-mouthed craving fresh-water tributes replenishing stocks dwindling fast never sated always thirsty feeding monstrous appetites demanding constant sacrifices devouring streams sacrificing forests drowning mountains overwhelming plains flooding coastal-fringes losing battle eternally raging restless tides retreating calmer advancing quieter shorelines surrendering land-won dearly defending lost ground reclaimed painstakingly draining marshlands diverting rivers rerouting nature reclaiming wetlands cultivating wilderness civilizing savagery developing lands opening routes pioneering roads settling nomads populating desert wastes harvesting sand-dunes anch