<<564 Prompt>> Give me a combination of an experiment exercise and an activity for a 5th grade student learning geography. <<Prompt>>
<<564 Generation>> Try this simple experiment to see how much water is used in your home each day.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Choose three different days on which you will make a record of every time anyone uses any water at all, whether it be flushing the toilet, washing their hands or face, taking a bath or shower, having a drink, making tea/coffee, etc., as well as using the dishwasher/washing machine/hosepipe/garden sprinkler/outdoor tap (if these apply).
\item Make sure that everyone in the house knows what they have got to do so that no-one forgets!
\end{enumerate}  

Now work out:
\begin {enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item the total amount of water used during those three days;
\item divide by 3 to get the daily average usage;  
\item multiply this number by 365 to find the yearly usage. 
\end {enumerate}

You can also compare the results from each of the three chosen days – were there many differences? Can you explain why some days use more than others?

If you live near a river, go down to take a look at it after heavy rain when the level may still be high, but probably falling fast.

Write notes about anything else you notice along the banks where the floodwaters reached, including damage caused by moving water. If you are able to visit again later, note any further changes since your previous visits. How long did it take before things returned to normal? Did plants grow quickly once more? Was wildlife soon back to its former state? Were any buildings restored/repaired/or left ruined? What other signs could you observe showing the effects of flooding? For example, consider changed courses of rivers/tributaries/banks/corners, or new lakes/lagoons formed where the waters rose above natural dams such as coastal cliffs or sand dunes blocking off low land behind them like lagoons. Or maybe areas below bridges/diversions/spillways/dams became blocked with debris carried downstream creating shallow pools attracting birds/fish/plants/insects. You might even spot small settlements created around oases filled up following storms! Keep track until full recovery then share findings verbally/pictures/notebooks with class. Discuss possible solutions proposed locally/internationally addressing problems arising due excessive rains leading towards disaster preparation plans emergency funds relief organizations volunteer groups providing shelter food medical aid psychological support. Finally propose ways people living far away help mitigate negative impacts felt globally yet cannot travel assist directly themselves physically without huge costs risks involved transporting goods resources across oceans continents! Think creatively involve whole school raise money awareness educate peers stop sending items won't reach affected places timely manner focus energy spreading news inciting actions taken elsewhere inspiring action response times matter greatly saving lives properties ecosystems worldwide!!   \paragraph{Questions} Answer these questions based on your observations and data collection.
