<<573 Generation>> \( 30 + 10 = \) count on in tens.

\begin{equation*}
    \_\_\_ = \text{\hspace{-0.5cm} the answer is }
\end{equATION*}

Count back to check your calculation:
\( 40 - 10 - 20 - 30 = \)

The number sentence \( 30+10=40 \) shows that when you take away ten from forty, you get thirty. Counting forward by ones can also prove this:

Forty minus one equals Thirty-nine.
Thirty-nine minus one more makes Thirty-eight.
