<<628 Prompt>> Give me a combination of a word problem and an arithmetic exercise for a 6th grade student learning mathematics. It should be an exercise on percentage calculation. It should be a with Answer. <<Prompt>>
<<628 Generation>> A school club had 500 members in January. The number decreased by \(10\%\) at the end of February, increased by \(15\% \)at the end March and finally decreased again by \(5\%\). How many were there at the beginning of April?

\subsection*{Answers}
The number of club members at the start of April is 536.

To find this answer:
\begin{itemize}
    \item First calculate \(10 \% \text { decrease } = (100-10)\%=90 \% \)
    \[ \Rightarrow 500 \times(0.9)=450 \]
    \end{itemIZE}

Then calculate \(15 % increase= (100+15)% =115% \)

\( [ \Rightarrow   450 \times (1.15)=517.50 ] \)


Finally calculate \(5 % \text{decrease} =(100 - 5 )\% =95\%   \)

\[ [ \therefore  517. 50 \times 0. 95 =493.25] \]

Round up to whole numbers since you cannot have fractions of people. This gives us our final count as follows:

January: 500; End of Feb.: 500 x 0.900 = 450;
End of Mar.: 450 X 1.150 = 517.5 ;
Start Apr.: 517 x 950 = 491.75, rounded up that's about 492 persons. However when using percentages we round each step which leads to slight differences due to rounding errors.
So although following these steps exactly will yield 491 after rounding from 491 point seven five it’s still more accurate than saying “roughly” or estimating without calculation.
But if your teacher uses exact figures then just remember every time you apply a percent change multiply by (100 ± Rate)/100 before moving onto next part! And don’t forget the order matters here too – always do decreases first because they’re essentially “less than one hundred percents”. Got all those steps down? Great now go solve some problems and show off what ya know!
