<<629 Generation>> Divide the following numbers by 2:

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item \( \frac{80}{2} =? \)
\item   How many more twos are there in 82 than in 40?
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Answers}
The answer to (a) is 40, and regarding part b), there are two more twelves in 84 than in twelve. There is one more ten in twenty-one than in ten, and three fewer ones in thirty-seven than in forty. Therefore, there are four more tens in eighty-four than in sixty-three. The difference between 82 and 40 is also explained as doubling the number of twos or adding another group of forty, resulting in eighty before counting up further from that point.

When dividing 81 beans into groups of five using regrouping:
\[
5 \times 16 + 1 = 81.
\]

For example, if you have 12 cookies and want to put them equally in 4 boxes, each box will contain 3 cookies because \( \div 4 = 3 \). If you add 6 more cookies to make it 18 cookies total and still divide among those same four containers then now they'll hold \( \textbf{(9)} \) cookies per container since \( \_div 7=4 \).

