If you want to get rid of \( y, \) simply add the two sides together so they cancel each other out like below.

Thus,

\[ x + y + x - y  =-6+8.\]

Adding those gives,

\[ 2x = 20,\] 

Which means,

\[x =10.\]  

This way, now if you substitute \( x = 10 \), back into your first equation where \( x +y = -6,\) you could find \( y : \)

So,

\[ y = -16. \]

And thus,

\( x =10 \) & \( y =-16.\)  

You can also check yourself here as well using both equations!

\section*{\large Method 2 (Elimination)}
Another method would involve multiplying either the first or second equation by a certain value such that adding them cancels one variable while keeping the other intact! This process is called elimination because you “eliminate” one variable from the system of equations.

