<<656 Generation>> After giving away \( \frac{1}{5} \) of his stamps, John had 76 stamps left.

How many stamps did he give away?

\subsection*{Answers}
He gave away 19 stamps. He originally had 95 stamps before giving some away. To find out how many stamps he gave away, we calculate:
\[ 95 - 76 = 19 \text{stamps}. \] 
Another method is to solve the equation:
\[ x + 76  = 95   \]
Subtracting 76 from both sides gives us:
\[x =  19 .\]  
So, he gave 19 away. Alternatively you can multiply by inverse calculation as follows:

\( 76 \times 5 = 380,\) then divide that number by four.
\( \dfrac {380 }{4}= 95 \)
Therefore, subtract the remaining stamps (76), and you will get your answer which equals 19. That’s it! You are done. The second way would be dividing 76 by 4 first.
\(  \dfract{76}{4}=19    \)

Multiply this result with five, and there you go!

\( 19\times 5=95    \) 

Now subtract again, or just take 19 – one fifth of ninety-five. There you got it!
