Maya writing has been found carved onto stone buildings and pottery, as well as painted onto books made from paper created from tree bark (these papers are called codices). The Spanish destroyed most of the Maya books but three remain.

The Maya developed an alphabet with more than 700 symbols – much bigger than ours! Some show objects such as animals and food; other signs indicate numbers, sounds and actions. Using this system, scribes could write down laws, scientific knowledge, myths and even daily events recorded by astronomers tracking stars and planets across the sky. They also left records showing which foods were harvested during different months. These help us learn what life was like back then!

