<<693 Generation>> In Ancient Roman times, what did the word ‘placidus' mean?  
\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item quiet and peaceful  
\item loud and noisy  
\end{enumerate}  

[STRDGM] A diagram showing various parts of an ancient Roman home including rooms labeled as 'Cubiculum,' 'Triclinium,' 'Exedra,' 'Atrium.' The house is divided into sections with different activities indicated in each section. [STPDGM]   % Page break after this question to avoid confusion about which image goes with which question.
\begin {figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\text width]{house-diagram.jpg}
    \caption {An illustration depicting various areas within an ancient roman dwelling. }
\ end {figure}

\subsection*{Answers}
The correct answer is \alpha. Placidus means "quiet" or "peaceful." It's also where we get our modern English words like placid from. Words that sound similar include sedate (from sedare meaning to calm), irate (enraged) and donate (give). You can use these words in sentences such as: I'm feeling rather irate today; she was very generous when she donated all her clothes. What other Latin roots do you know? Find three more examples.\\

You could explore how some of these words have changed over time by looking up their meanings online using sites like Etymology Online - etymonline.com. For example look at the entry on donate. Where does it come from originally?
% Another page break before next questions 
How has its usage developed since then?

We learn many new things every day but not everything sticks. We forget lots too – probably most things actually!
Learning something once isn't usually enough to make sure you remember it forever though so try repeating information out loud five minutes later and again one hour later.

Make notes on flash cards for important terms you need to memorise or write them down several times yourself if they are hard to spell because repetition helps us commit knowledge long term. Read your classmate’s work aloud too – saying stuff makes stronger memories than just reading silently. Good luck with remembering key facts now that exams loom near!! Try making mnemonics (memory aids.) These help trigger recall especially useful during tests/exams when faced questions requiring lists/names/dates etc., create rhymes/associations linking bits info together like “Every good boy deserves fudge” representing musical notes E G B D F on lines staff paper music theory lesson learned way back elementary school days still remembered perfectly clear decades later thanks clever teacher finding ways stick lessons minds students young old alike!!! Using acronyms another method aiding memory think sentence/word per letter abbreviation want associate easily accessible reminding oneself needed details quickly efficiently under pressure situation quiz competition sports event anything demands quick thinking action reaction speed matters greatly impacts success achieved end goal pursued diligently perseveringly reaching full potential everyone possessing equal opportunities succeed should utilize tools available maximize chance achieving greatness recognizing importance education applying life beyond classroom setting real world applications countless benefits derived simply paying attention classes taking place learning process itself enjoyable experience wholeheartedly embracing journey growth development character shaping future generations leaders innovators thinkers problem solvers dreamers believers achievers striving excellence relentless pursuit perfection settling mediocrity accepting status quo remaining stagnant idle unproductive wasting precious gift life granted us living moment fully present engaged attentive prepared seize opportunity knocks door embrace challenge