\paragraph{Itinerary Planning Exercise}
Imagine you live in Budapest, which is located in central-eastern Europe. You want to visit some friends who now live in Oslo, Norway. How do you plan to get there?

Plan your journey using maps.

You could travel by train, bus, car, bicycle, aeroplane, ferry boat – or any combination of those methods!

What route would you take? Which countries and major cities would you pass through on your way? What form of transport would you use each step of the way? Would you prefer one type of transport rather than others? Why?  

Now think about planning a trip from where you live to school. What method(s) of getting to school do you like best? Say why.  

Finally, choose somewhere quite far away outside Europe that you might like to visit one day, perhaps New Zealand. Again work out possible ways of making that long journey, considering all forms of transport mentioned earlier plus horseback riding or camel! Think about preferences here also. Explain them briefly.  

Prepare three separate plans for class display based upon the following criteria:

\begin {enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item Plan a detailed itinerary from Budapest to Oslo, specifying the mode of transport, route, and stops along the way.
\item Outline the preferred method of traveling from home to school, explaining reasons behind the choice.
\itim Describe potential journeys from Europe to New Zealand, evaluating options involving different transports and personal preferences.
\iteM Prepare visual aids like diagrams or charts to illustrate your findings for classroom presentation.
