<<804 Generation>> There are \_\_ more boys than girls.

[STRDGM] A group of children with 5 boys on one side [STPDGM]
[STRDGMA] On another side there is only one girl visible who standing near to them. [STPDOGM] 
[STRDMG] In total there are six boys and three girls in this image. [STDGM] 

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item How many fewer girls than boys?
\end{enumerate}  

\begin {figure}[h!]
    \centering
   \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{images/girls_and_boys.png}
   % Use the width parameter to scale the image as needed.
\end {figure}

The number sentence can be written as \(2 +? = 6\) or \(? - 3 = 2\).

Solve it using addition/subtraction:

\( 2 + \underline{\hspace{1cm}} = 7 \)

or

\( \underline{-9pt} – 3  = 11 \) 

Fewer means you need to find out how much less something is compared to other things. To do that subtract smaller amount from bigger one. That will give us an answer we were looking for. Answer must be positive since negative would mean "few" instead. Which does not make any sense here because then question wouldn't ask about difference but rather position relative each other thing being counted/found/measured/etc... Hence correct representation through math formula should look like following one:

Answer = Bigger Quantity - Smaller One  
Answer = Amount Of Boys - Amount Of Girls  
