Imagine you’re advising farmers whose lands extend very close to retreating cliff edges. They ask for suggestions on making best use possible remaining space available them longer period.

In a sentence or two, tell the farmer what advice you'd offer concerning usage strategies maximize productivity despite challenging circumstances posed gradual yet inevitable loss arable farm land moving forward future seasons ahead... Remember keep concise clear recommendations practical easy implement given context scenario presented question prompt itself essentially asking solution ideas help mitigate impact slow progression reduce effective agricultural output gradually decreasing total usable acreage.

If you lived on the coast, especially on cliffs, you might see small pieces break off occasionally. This breaking-off is called cliff erosion. Over thousands of years, cliff erosion makes the whole piece of land get smaller and smaller. Some cliffs move backwards really slowly, less than 1 metre per year. Others move much quicker. Sometimes big bits fall off all at once. When this happens we call it a landslide. Landslips happen when flat areas of land slip seawards. There isn't always anything left on the cliff tops. But sometimes there are villages, roads, railways, golf courses and even factories built right up to the edge. These often end up in the sea! So, it's obviously important to try to stop cliff erosion happening. Engineers build walls at the bottom of cliffs to prevent the base getting washed away. However, it doesn't usually stop erosion completely and the cliffs continue to crumble away bit by bit. Still, the defences do slow the process down a lot. At least people don’t lose their homes and businesses straightaway! And there aren't many train crashes either!
It wasn't until I got home that Mum told me she saw something odd. She said that lots of men came running past her window shouting urgently to get back from the edge of the cliffs NOW! They were pointing upwards and my mum looked up as well. As she reached the door she heard a loud rumbling sound and turned round to watch half the garden disappear into thin air! It seemed that the cliff we walked on had broken off altogether taking plants, fences and sheds tumbling into the depths below!! Luckily no-one was hurt thank goodness. We went outside later and measured the drop – it had grown ten times bigger overnight. All the lovely flowers fell down and were lost forever beneath giant boulders lying shattered at bottom. Nothing except scrappy weeds grows anymore – it became impossible grow vegetables safely. Mum felt sorry seeing beautiful greenery gone like magic. On the good side nobody died and triffic defence walls prevented trains derailing saving numerous passengers aboard. My little brother Tom was excited hearing real-life stories told afterwards playing happily amongst wreckage while dad fixed broken wheelbarrow… I wish nothing broke that day... Hehehe…
I remember reading news articles telling sad tales about cliffs eaten away leaving houses teetering over empty spaces – owners crying having nowhere stay – eventually <<Generation>>
