<<843 Generation>> Write two reasons why the cities of Dubai and Abu Dhabi are built so far inland, rather than on the coast. Think about trade and fishing as you write your answers! Then look at an atlas or globe to check that you have written correct answers! Write down any corrections you need to make. Now think more carefully about this question.
\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item Why did traders not want their ships to travel too far up into the Arabian Gulf?  
\item Would it be easier or harder to sail in and out of ports near Dubai and near Basra?
\end{enumerate}   Explain how these things affected where people chose to build the cities along the southern shore of the Arabian Peninsula (or Persian Gulf). Remember to use evidence from maps if possible. Finally explain why the rulers of Abu Dhabi decided to move its port further out to sea! What special equipment will they need? Look again at your map – is there another country with similar problems? Where has this country placed some of its main towns and cities? Give three examples. Can you suggest one way these countries could solve the problem without moving the cities? You might find useful information by looking up ‘canal' in a dictionary or encyclopedia. Include this extra information in your explanation. Don't forget to give examples! When you were younger, do you remember thinking that grown-up clothes looked funny because they didn't fit properly? Many pupils used to live close to school but now many children's parents work away from home, often abroad. Children may even spend part of each year living with grandparents who stay nearer to the schools. It takes time and money to keep sending them backwards and forwards between Mum's and Dad's homes or between family members when the school term starts and finishes. Some families also prefer their children to board during the week because both parents have very demanding jobs. So boarding schools are becoming increasingly popular for several different reasons connected with modern life today. The same applies to universities. A few years ago most students lived quite close to university campuses; nowadays increasing numbers come from all over the world. This means that nearly every higher education institution offers places in Halls of Residence. These are usually situated next door to the campus buildings which saves petrol and reduces traffic congestion around the area. Because such accommodation can only offer single rooms and shared facilities like kitchens, bathrooms and lounges, students try hard to get accepted by the ones provided nearest to the lecture theatres and libraries since the best halls always fill quickly once applications open after results are published in late summer. For those less fortunate, other types exist including private hostels, lodgings and flat shares, although these cost much more per month and don't include meals. Students generally share kitchen duties and pay according to what ingredients everyone uses. However, sharing the rent helps reduce costs while helping new friends settle into unfamiliar surroundings until they gain confidence going out alone or meeting local people through sports clubs, gyms, church groups etc. During vacation periods students return home unless working locally to save rail fares back and forth. Living in residences makes academic lives easier though because students don't waste hours commuting daily across large city areas before reaching lectures then library study afterwards. Also they avoid wasting energy cooking just for themselves especially when preferring simple snacks instead of proper dinners requiring greater preparation effort! As soon as young adults finish exams at end May/beginning June, those whose courses continue following Michaelmas Term go straight home immediately leaving everything behind whereas others remaining longer pack suitcases preparing departures towards final weeks leading September/October returning bringing items needing replacement/renewing/fresh stock replenishments/laundry cleaning etcetera. While residing inside uni accommodations there isn’t really necessity maintaining entire house independently hence saving expenditure indirectly cutting fuel bills via public transport reduction likewise conserving household item purchases given sharing among peers already owning required appliances minimizes personal shopping lists regarding grocery expenses. Overall despite initial impressions suggesting otherwise staying within educational premises tends result significant advantages financially socially academically. In fact I know somebody studying overseas currently feels safer knowing she resides amongst classmates rather navigating completely foreign roads alone plus having assistance readily available whenever needed whether intellectually relating course material or personally coping homesickness adjusting cultural differences simultaneously broadening understanding perspectives worldwide through interactions fellow global scholars. She sends photos showing corridors filled decorated doors indicating vibrant diversity reflected visually illustrating rich multicultural atmosphere created blending international influences generating unique exciting environment motivating enthusiastic individuals pursue knowledge unafraid ask questions explore ideas freely exchanging thoughts sparking inspiration creativity blossoming minds eager grow mature confident visionary leaders tomorrow shaping brighter futures collectively breaking barriers forging lifelong friendships memories forever cherished marking beginning incredible journey embarked together hand-in-hand. Your friend is right - being in Hall lets her enjoy aspects making uni-life enjoyable like getting involved societies joining clubs attending events volunteering participating competitions playing sport contributing community radio newspaper writing articles creating art showcasing talents performing stage acting singing dancing reciting poetry discussing books reading writing stories composing music recording songs producing films directing plays choreographing dance routines designing fashion collections painting murals sculpturing models carving wood crafting jewelry weaving tapestry knitting garments sewing clothes gardening growing plants nursing animals tending nature reserves managing recycling schemes raising charity funds promoting environmental awareness organizing campaigns supporting good causes collecting donations distributing aid materials delivering food clothing med