Now that we've learned about conditions at home, what do you think life was like onboard a caravel? Think about how much room there was; whether anyone got seasick; and what food they ate - remember that ice wouldn't keep fish fresh for long so most sailors didn't eat fish at sea... \\

One major problem faced by Portuguese explorers was finding enough men willing to sail down the west coast of Africa into unknown waters. Many were frightened because maps showed the world ending just beyond West Africa! To encourage more volunteers, Henry began building a small settlement near Lagos, Portugal. He named this village Terceira and offered land grants to those who sailed with him. One volunteer sailor/explorer, Diogo Gomes found one way to overcome fears about sailing past the western edge of the world. In 1445 he discovered two groups of islands off northwest Africa: Madeira and the Azores. These discoveries meant sailors could stop and take on supplies before continuing their journey south along the African coastline towards India. They also gave Europeans places where they could grow sugar cane to produce sugar, helping develop these industries. By now, other countries had begun exploring too. Soon many rulers wanted to find a route to Asia themselves rather than use the Ottomans. Most hoped to reach China, but finding a faster route became urgent after news spread of Chinese Admiral Zheng He reaching East Africa in 1418.\\

Life On Board Caravels Explorers used light ships known as caravels. Only sixty-five metres long, weighing only 100 tonnes, these made excellent exploration vessels since they were fast and maneuverable. Their speed helped them escape danger while their ability to turn quickly allowed crews to navigate narrow channels between sandbanks where larger boats couldn't travel. They drew less water so could go closer to shore and explore rivers safely without getting stuck in shallow areas or crashing against river banks during high winds/tides etc., ensuring smoother journeys overall!

